    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "makes sense. blood meal sounds like the way to go then. i'm a bit worried about the application rate though, i dont want to burn the roots if i overdo it. is there a specific way i should incorporate it into the soil so it doesn't just sit on top, or is there anything else i should be looking out for in the soil structure itself?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For optimal nitrogen uptake without risking root burn, gently work blood meal into the top 2-3 inches of soil using a garden fork or tiller. Creating a fine, even distribution of the blood meal will prevent it from being washed away by rain and promote efficient absorption by your kale's roots. When planting, ensure your kale seeds or transplants aren't buried too deeply under the blood meal, suggesting you should plant according to seed packet instructions, or dig a shallow hole for your transplants with the base just below the furrow where the blood meal was distributed. Additionally, since you're concerned about the soil structure, ensure it's loose and well-aerated for better root growth and nutrient accessibility. Periodic soil testing is sensible, not just for pH, but also to monitor nitrogen levels and ensure that your intervention is indeed effective without causing an imbalanced nutrient profile in the soil.",
      "source": "target",
